[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Threads: Creating and Managing Threads"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Java, a thread is a lightweight sub-process, a separate path of execution within a program. Multithreading allows concurrent execution of two or more parts of a program for maximum utilization of the CPU. Java provides built-in support for multithreaded programming. This tutorial will guide you through creating and managing threads in Java."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Threads"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"There are two primary ways to create threads in Java:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"By extending the `Thread` class."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"By implementing the `Runnable` interface."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Extending the Thread Class"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a thread by extending the `Thread` class, you need to:"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create a class that extends the `Thread` class."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Override the `run()` method. This method contains the code that will be executed by the thread."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create an instance of your thread class."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Call the `start()` method on the thread object to begin execution.  Do not call `run()` directly, as this will execute the code in the current thread, not a new thread."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"class MyThread extends Thread {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread " + Thread.currentThread().getId() + " is running");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread thread1 = new MyThread();
        MyThread thread2 = new MyThread();
        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the above example, `MyThread` extends the `Thread` class and overrides the `run()` method to print a message. The `main()` method creates two instances of `MyThread` and starts them using the `start()` method.  `Thread.currentThread().getId()` returns the unique identifier for the currently executing thread."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing the Runnable Interface"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a thread by implementing the `Runnable` interface, you need to:"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create a class that implements the `Runnable` interface."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement the `run()` method. This method contains the code that will be executed by the thread."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create an instance of your `Runnable` class."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create a `Thread` object, passing your `Runnable` instance to the `Thread` constructor."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Call the `start()` method on the `Thread` object to begin execution."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"class MyRunnable implements Runnable {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread " + Thread.currentThread().getId() + " is running");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyRunnable myRunnable = new MyRunnable();
        Thread thread1 = new Thread(myRunnable);
        Thread thread2 = new Thread(myRunnable);
        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();
    }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `MyRunnable` implements the `Runnable` interface and implements the `run()` method. In the `main()` method, a `MyRunnable` object is created, and then two `Thread` objects are created, each using the same `MyRunnable` instance. Calling `start()` on the `Thread` objects initiates the execution of the `run()` method in separate threads."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Managing Threads"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java provides several methods for managing threads. Some of the important methods are:"},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`start()`: Starts the thread.  This calls the `run()` method in a new thread of execution."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`run()`: The entry point for the thread. Contains the code to be executed."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`join()`: Waits for the thread to die.  The calling thread will block until the thread on which `join()` is called completes its execution."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`sleep(long milliseconds)`: Causes the current thread to suspend execution for a specified period of time."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`interrupt()`: Interrupts the thread.  This sets the interrupt status of the thread. The thread can check this status using `isInterrupted()` and respond accordingly, typically by exiting the `run()` method."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`isAlive()`: Checks if the thread is alive (i.e., has started and not yet finished)."},{"order":32,"type":"subheading","text":"Thread States"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"A thread can be in one of several states:"},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"NEW: The thread has been created but not yet started."},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"RUNNABLE: The thread is ready to run. It may be running or waiting for its turn to run."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"BLOCKED: The thread is waiting for a monitor lock to enter a synchronized block/method."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"WAITING: The thread is waiting indefinitely for another thread to perform a particular action (e.g., calling `wait()` on an object and waiting for `notify()` or `notifyAll()`)."},{"order":38,"type":"bullet_point","text":"TIMED_WAITING: The thread is waiting for a specified amount of time (e.g., calling `sleep()` or `wait(long timeout)`)."},{"order":39,"type":"bullet_point","text":"TERMINATED: The thread has completed its execution."},{"order":40,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Using join() and sleep()"},{"order":41,"type":"code","text":"class MyThread extends Thread {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            System.out.println("Thread " + Thread.currentThread().getId() + " started");
            Thread.sleep(2000); // Simulate some work
            System.out.println("Thread " + Thread.currentThread().getId() + " finished");
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println("Thread " + Thread.currentThread().getId() + " interrupted");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread thread1 = new MyThread();
        MyThread thread2 = new MyThread();
        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();

        try {
            thread1.join(); // Wait for thread1 to finish
            thread2.join(); // Wait for thread2 to finish
            System.out.println("Both threads have finished");
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println("Main thread interrupted");
        }
    }
}"},{"order":42,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `main` thread waits for both `thread1` and `thread2` to complete their execution using the `join()` method. The `sleep()` method simulates some work being done by the threads. The `InterruptedException` is caught in case the thread is interrupted while sleeping."},{"order":43,"type":"subheading","text":"Thread Priorities"},{"order":44,"type":"paragraph","text":"Each thread has a priority, which is an integer value between `Thread.MIN_PRIORITY` (1) and `Thread.MAX_PRIORITY` (10). The default priority is `Thread.NORM_PRIORITY` (5). Thread priorities are used by the thread scheduler to decide which thread to run next. However, the effect of thread priorities can vary depending on the operating system and JVM implementation. Setting thread priorities does not guarantee that a higher-priority thread will always run before a lower-priority thread."},{"order":45,"type":"code","text":"Thread myThread = new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Thread running with priority: " + Thread.currentThread().getPriority());
});

myThread.setPriority(Thread.MAX_PRIORITY);
myThread.start();"},{"order":46,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code sets the priority of `myThread` to the maximum priority before starting it. The output will show the thread's priority."},{"order":47,"type":"subheading","text":"Daemon Threads"},{"order":48,"type":"paragraph","text":"A daemon thread is a low-priority thread that runs in the background to perform tasks such as garbage collection. When all non-daemon threads finish their execution, the JVM exits, even if daemon threads are still running. You can set a thread as a daemon thread by calling the `setDaemon(true)` method before starting the thread."},{"order":49,"type":"code","text":"Thread daemonThread = new Thread(() -> {
    while (true) {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(1000); // Simulate background task
            System.out.println("Daemon thread running");
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            break;
        }
    }
});

daemonThread.setDaemon(true);
daemonThread.start();

// Main thread finishes quickly
System.out.println("Main thread finished");"},{"order":50,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `daemonThread` is set as a daemon thread. The main thread finishes quickly, and the JVM will exit, even if the daemon thread is still running its loop. Note that `setDaemon()` must be called before `start()`."},{"order":51,"type":"subheading","text":"Synchronization (Brief Overview)"},{"order":52,"type":"paragraph","text":"When multiple threads access shared resources, it's crucial to synchronize their access to prevent data corruption and race conditions. Java provides several mechanisms for synchronization, including:"},{"order":53,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Synchronized methods and blocks: Using the `synchronized` keyword to protect critical sections of code."},{"order":54,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Locks: Using `Lock` interfaces (e.g., `ReentrantLock`) to provide more flexible locking mechanisms."},{"order":55,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Atomic variables: Using classes like `AtomicInteger` and `AtomicLong` for thread-safe operations on single variables."},{"order":56,"type":"paragraph","text":"A detailed discussion of synchronization is beyond the scope of this introductory tutorial, but it's a fundamental concept in multithreaded programming."},{"order":57,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":58,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial covered the basics of creating and managing threads in Java. You learned how to create threads by extending the `Thread` class and implementing the `Runnable` interface, and how to manage threads using methods like `start()`, `join()`, `sleep()`, and `interrupt()`. Understanding multithreading is essential for developing concurrent and efficient Java applications."}]
